{"chunk_id": "eb34ed71e2a8", "text": "In heaven, King Yayati is honored by the gods and occasionally visits the realm of Brahman. Indra asks him what counsel he gave his son Puru when the son accepted Yayati\u2019s old age and received the kingdom. Yayati recounts a long moral instruction: rule justly, restrain anger, forgive injuries, avoid cruel speech, emulate the virtuous, and treat kindness, charity, and gentle words as the highest worship of the gods. Later, when Indra asks Yayati about his ascetic merit, Yayati proudly claims no equal among men, gods, or sages. Indra rebukes this arrogance, declaring that pride and disregard for others diminish his virtue, causing him to fall from heaven. As Yayati descends, the sage-king Ashtaka sees him blazing like the sun and welcomes him among the wise, affirming the sacred duty of hospitality and support among the virtuous.\nIndra questions Yayati, whose pride causes his fall from heaven; he asks to fall among the virtuous and is encountered by the sage Ashtaka.\nThemes: pride and humility, fall from heaven, virtue and speech ethics, kingship counsel, divine judgment", "vector": [0.015193134546279907, -0.023894047364592552, 0.0007864287472330034, -0.010835983790457249, 0.004491010215133429, 0.0380430743098259, -0.02068140357732773, -0.0059634726494550705, -0.012428920716047287, -0.014497061260044575, 0.00271568913012743, -0.02172551304101944, 0.011873400770127773, 0.01074228249490261, -0.015541170723736286, -0.0027207087259739637, 0.040104519575834274, -0.002986755920574069, 0.027146851643919945, -0.0030553594697266817, -0.0044675846584141254, 0.004661682061851025, -0.0171207208186388, 0.009965892881155014, -0.013760830275714397, -0.0028796677943319082, 0.022783007472753525, -0.011866708286106586, 0.017870338633656502, -0.027976784855127335, -0.007194986566901207, 0.006887108087539673, 0.0013235429069027305, 0.02026643604040146, -0.0037480860482901335, 0.004370535723865032, -0.011612373404204845, 0.005695751868188381, 0